152 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (  A memory leak refers to a situation where a computer program allocates memory \(space\) for temporary use, but fails to release it when it's no longer needed. It's like renting a storage unit but forgetting to return the key when you're done using it. As a result, that space remains unavailable for other tasks, leading to inefficient memory usage. Over time, if memory leaks accumulate, they can deplete the available memory resources, causing the program or system to slow down, become unresponsive, or even crash.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [0]
>>
